I was about six months pregnant with my  daughter and a neighbor asked me Julie  we go back to work after the baby is  born all I could think was are you  kidding me of course I'm gonna go back  to work after the baby's born my  identity was tied to my career success  but it was only a few short months later  I was holding my daughter I don't lie  could think was are you kidding me of  course I'm not going back to work how  was I to reconcile these two identities  how was I going to define my own  happiness I realized then that the paved  road in front of me didn't lead to my  happiness I was going to have to blaze  my own Trail now what do I mean by that  I mean societal norms had left women  choosing between staying at home and  career oriented but I guess I just  didn't see the world is so black and  white and I needed to figure out the way  to my happiness so I've spent the last  13 years blazing my own trail I've  co-founded a company and I've raised a  family and the trail is far from  complete but I've learned a few things  along the way I've learned that first  and foremost you must define your own  happiness ignore the critics I've  learned that creative solutions are  oftentimes the key to success and I've  learned that it's worth it it's worth  the blood sweat and tears in order to  find your own happiness so how will you  define your happiness will you blaze  your own Trail I stand here before you  the experiences of a mom but the reality  is that dad's oftentimes have the same  questions not to mention the need to  love and support the mothers in their  lives  understanding what would make me happy  was a journey I spent long hours that a  hedge fund and trying to balance that  with a family I was miserable and when I  tried to just stay at home I wasn't  content either I needed to find a way  through for a creative solution my  husband and i co-founded a company and  the naysayers that we were crazy to  start a family with a young to start a  company with a young family but they  didn't share my priorities as my own  boss I had to leverage to create value  and a license to pursue my ideas but at  the same time I had flexibility to work  half days and be at home with my  daughter we kept strict rules between  five and eight pm was sacred we had  dinner together and we traveled very  little and yes there were days where we  had to tell clients we simply couldn't  make it to a meeting one crucial and  important meeting we missed in order to  be at our son's kindergarten graduation  as I stand here now and I look back with  hindsight being twenty-twenty my choices  seem obvious to me let me be clear it  wasn't easy we sacrificed company growth  for our family priorities and there are  so many times where I felt like I sucked  both at being a mom and a manager who  are today a student but we stayed  committed to our path now there's a term  for this lifestyle choice the mom  preneur lisa stone rock this model after  the birth of her son much to everyone's  surprise she left her job in the  newsroom at CNN in order to blaze her  own path her creative solution blogger  com now again the naysayer said she's  crazy women aren't going to go online  but she leveraged the fact that she was  a single mom to a one and a half year  old in Silicon Valley to know that this  really was a possibility but this talk  is not about being an entrepreneur  working full-time working part-time or  staying at home it's about knowing if  you keep an openness to creative  solutions the possibilities are endless  so there was another pivotal moments in  my life hi had three school-age children  I was on the verge of selling my company  and I had been accepted to the GSB on  many counts I had accomplished what I  set out to do it was a day before April  orientation and I found out I was  pregnant and holy crap twins as I began  to look up at my new reality I was  prepared to set aside my lifelong dream  of attending Stanford I was a mom I knew  what I had to do and I was prepared to  do it when we sat down to tell her other  children about the twins entrance into  our lives my daughter then 11 years old  looked up at me and said mom you're  still going to go to Stanford right you  have to  so you stand here now blazing through my  own doubts blazing a path is the mom  with the most number of children at the  GSB perhaps ever but but this was my  dream and I knew I had to do it when my  daughter asked me I knew I'd have to  figure out a way to come and I'm here  Teresa Taylor in her book the balance  myth says blending your life together is  really about being authentic all the  time I don't think of myself as someone  with two different lives or two  different places to be this is me this  is who I am I love this notion I  subscribe to it completely my light my  family is a part of everything I do  they're a part of my authentic self so  when I started writing this talk a few  months ago Stanford assign me a speech  coach I knew even then that I wanted to  include my family as part of the talk  excitedly told him my plan but he was  less than enthusiastic but Julie so much  could go wrong and he's right my twins  might get up here and start crying my  teenage daughter she's unlikely to smile  and my boys well they could poke at each  other in front of all of you but how was  I to get up here and give a talk about  integrating work and family while  ignoring the naysayers without doing  exactly that so let me introduce to you  this is josh Helen max Ryan Sam Luke and  my niece Claire without whose help I  wouldn't be here I'm so glad they're  here because I need them and I need you  to see them so now when you see me on  campus with perhaps some avocado on my  sleeve or bags under my eyes you'll know  why but but in this avocados filled  sleepless world I'm pursuing my dreams  and no I don't have everything I miss a  lot of things at the GSB and  occasionally I missed some things at  home too but I'm happy so your paths  won't look the same as mine and okay no  clapping for that part and it likely  will look different than even what you  expect but in those moments when you're  facing the paved road in front of you  and you realize that it doesn't lead to  your happiness know that if you trip  blaze your own path the possibilities  are endless figure out what feeds your  passion refuse to accept can't or not  possible how will you define your  happiness will you blaze your trail  ok
 

